    <div id="sC-component-footnotes" class="ts-footnote-2">
<p><a name="footnote1"></a><sup>1</sup> I  would like to thank James Egelhof, Jacqueline Yen and Samuel Zief for their  assistance in the preparation of these remarks, Vivek Manjunath for his  assistance with data, and colleagues in the Federal Reserve System for numerous  insightful comments and suggestions. </p>
  <p><a name="footnote2"></a><sup>2</sup> See Powell, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/powell20180508a.htm" target="_blank">Monetary  Policy Influences on Global Financial Conditions and International Capital  Flows</a>, May 8, 2018.</p>
<p><a name="footnote3"></a><sup>3</sup> The effective federal funds rate is calculated as a volume-weighted median of overnight  federal funds transactions reported by banks on the FR 2420 Report of Selected  Money Market Rates.  The New York Fed  publishes the EFFR for the prior business day, along with volumes and rate  percentiles, on its website daily at approximately 9:00 a.m. Eastern Time.  The EFFR is used as a measure of the typical  fed funds transaction and is the way the New York Fed&rsquo;s Open Market Trading  Desk (the Desk) measures its success in meeting the directive on interest rate  control from the FOMC.</p>
<p><a name="footnote4"></a><sup>4</sup> These remarks focus on dollar money markets, many of which are linked to LIBOR,  and do not discuss the reference rate itself.   Dudley, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2018/dud180524" target="_blank">The Transition  to a Robust Reference Rate Regime</a>, May 24, 2018, offers perspective on the  outlook for LIBOR as a reference rate.</p>
<p><a name="footnote5"></a><sup>5</sup> Potter, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2016/pot160222" target="_blank">Money  Markets after Liftoff: Assessment to Date and the Road Ahead</a>, February 22,  2016, provides a detailed discussion of the new framework. For a discussion of  the new and old frameworks see <a href="https://www.aeaweb.org/articles?id=10.1257/jep.29.4.177" target="_blank">Ihrig, Meade,  Weinbach (2015)</a>.</p>
<p><a name="footnote6"></a><sup>6</sup> See Logan, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2018/log180504" target="_blank">Operational  Perspectives on Monetary Policy Implementation: Panel Remarks on The Future of  the Central Bank Balance Sheet</a>, May 4, 2018, for a comparison of the  technical operation of the pre-crisis and current frameworks.</p>
<p><a name="footnote7"></a><sup>7</sup> This one day was December 31, 2015, when the federal funds rate printed at 20  basis points, or five basis points below the bottom of the FOMC&rsquo;s target  range.  Potter, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2016/pot160222" target="_blank">Money  Markets after Liftoff: Assessment to Date and the Road Ahead</a>, February 22,  2016, discusses this occasion.</p>
<p><a name="footnote8"></a><sup>8</sup> I  discussed dispersion as a means to evaluate the efficacy of monetary control  and transmission in an earlier speech.   These data suggest that the framework continues to perform quite well  along this dimension.  See Potter, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2017/pot170405" target="_blank">Money  Markets at a Crossroads: Policy Implementation at a Time of Structural Change</a>,  April 5, 2017.</p>
<p><a name="footnote9"></a><sup>9</sup> See Potter, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2017/pot171011" target="_blank">Gradual  and Predictable: Reducing the Size of the Federal Reserve&rsquo;s Balance Sheet</a>,  October 11, 2017, for a discussion of balance sheet normalization.</p>
<p><a name="footnote10"></a><sup>10</sup> See the FOMC&rsquo;s <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/FOMC_PolicyNormalization.pdf" target="_blank">Policy  Normalization Principles and Plans</a>, September 16, 2014.</p>
<p><a name="footnote11"></a><sup>11</sup> <a href="/markets/opolicy/operating_policy_170920" target="_blank">Statement  Regarding Reinvestment in Treasury Securities and Agency Mortgage-Backed  Securities</a>, September 20, 2017.</p>
<p><a name="footnote12"></a><sup>12</sup> Philadelphia Fed President Harker used this expression first in relation to  balance sheet normalization.</p>
<p><a name="footnote13"></a><sup>13</sup> <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/files/fomcminutes20180613.pdf" target="_blank">See  June 2018 FOMC minutes</a>. The Markets Group conducts small-value exercises in  both domestic and foreign financial markets for the purpose of testing  operational readiness. For additional discussion, see <a href="/medialibrary/media/markets/omo/omo2017-pdf.pdf" target="_blank">Domestic  Open Market Operations During 2017</a>, a report prepared by the Markets Group  of the New York Fed for the FOMC.</p>
<p><a name="footnote14"></a><sup>14</sup> This adjustment was discussed within the <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/monetarypolicy/fomcminutes20180502.htm" target="_blank">May  2018 FOMC minutes</a>.  </p>
<p><a name="footnote15"></a><sup>15</sup> The Federal Reserve Act provides the Federal Reserve with the authority to pay  interest on reserves (IOR).  In  Regulation D, the Board of Governors implemented this authority in the form of  two rates: interest on required reserves (IORR), and interest on excess  reserves (IOER).  Since late 2008, these  two rates have been set to the same value.</p>
<p><a name="footnote16"></a><sup>16</sup> The classic paper on this topic is <a href="https://www.jstor.org/stable/pdf/2325906.pdf" target="_blank">Poole (1968)</a>.  A number of recent efforts have been  undertaken to modernize the Poole model by adding interest on reserves and  other features of modern money markets.   Two notable examples of this are <a href="https://www.chicagofed.org/~/media/publications/working-papers/2018/wp2018-08-pdf.pdf" target="_blank">Schulhofer-Wohl  and Clouse (2018)</a> and <a href="/research/staff_reports/sr840" target="_blank">Afonso,  Armenter and Lester (2018)</a>. </p>
<p><a name="footnote17"></a><sup>17</sup> Alternatively, it could be that banks are more efficient in their balance sheet  management, which would be consistent with the lower volatility in overnight  rates we have seen at recent quarter-ends.   Bargaining dynamics within overnight money markets, which exhibit a  fairly high degree of concentration of both borrowing and lending, could also  have played a role in the rise over time in market rates relative to IOR.</p>
<p><a name="footnote18"></a><sup>18</sup> For a further discussion of the shape of the demand curve for reserves and its  implications for policy implementation, see Logan, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2018/log180504" target="_blank">Operational  Perspectives on Monetary Policy Implementation: Panel Remarks on The Future of  the Central Bank Balance Sheet</a>, May 4, 2018.</p>
 <p><a name="footnote19"></a><sup>19</sup> In the spring there was an increase in borrowing in the federal funds market above  IOR, but volume in the Eurodollar market decreased around the same time. </p>
<p><a name="footnote20"></a><sup>20</sup> For  example, there has been an expansion of access to centrally-cleared repo, through  the introduction of the GCF Repo Service&rsquo;s Centrally Cleared Institutional  Triparty Service (CCIT), which opened the centrally-cleared triparty repo  market to institutional cash lenders, and the growth of activity in the  centrally-cleared bilateral repo market by institutions being sponsored by  members of the Fixed Income Clearing Corporation&rsquo;s (FICC) Government Securities  Division.  Separately, some foreign  banking organizations that had been active borrowers in the offshore Eurodollar  market have shifted their borrowing in favor of domestic deposits that are  economically similar to Eurodollars.</p>
<p><a name="footnote21"></a><sup>21</sup> <a href="/medialibrary/media/research/epr/2018/epr_2018_pre-crisis-framework_sarkar.pdf" target="_blank">Kroeger,  McGowan, and Sarkar (2018)</a> provides detailed background on behavior of the  pre-crisis framework.</p>
<p><a name="footnote22"></a><sup>22</sup> The Regulation D reserve requirement is still binding for many smaller banks.</p>
<p><a name="footnote23"></a><sup>23</sup> It is important to note that these new regulatory and  behavioral factors are related to liquidity in general and not strictly about  reserves held in Federal Reserve accounts as in Regulation D.  Banks have many possible means for addressing  their requirements under the LCR, and the LCR rule does not impose any specific  requirement to hold central bank reserves.</p>
<p><a name="footnote24"></a><sup>24</sup> Potter, <a href="/newsevents/speeches/2016/pot160826" target="_blank">Discussion  of &lsquo;Evaluating Monetary Policy Operational Frameworks&rsquo; By Ulrich Bindseil</a>, August  26, 2016.  Discussions with banks suggest  both the incentives and operational preparedness for large scale interbank  unsecured transactions is low. </p>
<p><a name="footnote25"></a><sup>25</sup> The decrease in reserves is generally matched by an equal increase (as measured  by market value) in securities under most assumptions.  See Leonard, Martin and Potter, <a href="http://libertystreeteconomics.newyorkfed.org/2017/07/how-the-fed-changes-the-size-of-its-balance-sheet.html" target="_blank">How  the Fed Changes the Size of its Balance Sheet</a>,  July 10, 2017, for a discussion of some technical factors that could result in  the values not being exactly the same.   From an LCR perspective, reducing agency MBS holdings can affect banks&rsquo;  measured LCRs to a moderate extent because agency MBS are Level 2 HQLA and are  counted with an 85 percent weighting.  </p>
<p><a name="footnote26"></a><sup>26</sup> Quarles, <a href="https://www.federalreserve.gov/newsevents/speech/quarles20180504a.htm" target="_blank">Liquidity  Regulation and the Size of the Fed&rsquo;s Balance Sheet</a>, May 4, 2018.</p>
<p><a name="footnote27"></a><sup>27</sup> See <a href="https://www.bis.org/fsi/fsisummaries/lcr.htm" target="_blank">Liquidity Coverage  Ratio (LCR) – Executive Summary</a> and <a href="https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0304393217300910" target="_blank">Bech  and Keister</a> (2017).</p>
<p><a name="footnote28"></a><sup>28</sup> <a href="https://www.bis.org/publ/bcbs238.pdf" target="_blank">Basel III: The Liquidity Coverage  Ratio and liquidity risk monitoring tools</a>, page 73.</p>
<p><a name="footnote29"></a><sup>29</sup> In particular on June 20 and 21 our market intelligence and analysis of data  suggest that LCR-motivated borrowing from FHLBs by a few banks placed modest  upward pressure on traded rates in the federal funds market.  A contributing factor was a decline in funds  supplied into the market from the FHLBs, due to an increase in demand for  advances around the mid-month tax date.  Data  on historical traded rates in the Eurodollar market are published with a  quarterly lag as part of the New York Fed&rsquo;s Quarterly FR 2420 Summary  Statistics <a href="/medialibrary/media/markets/FR-2420-summary-statistics.xlsx" target="_blank">release</a>.</p>
<p><a name="footnote30"></a><sup>30</sup> Indeed, an alternative explanation for the movement of federal funds trading  further up in the range since lift-off could be FHLBs gaining more bargaining  power with banks as they observe counterparties being willing to trade at  higher rates. A good example of this was in December 2014 when temporary  testing of different offering rates on the ON RRP produced a persistent  increase in the level of federal funds trading. </p>
    </div>
         

</div>

